<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philitas of Cos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 340–c. 285 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Philetas of Cos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philitas of Cos was a Greek poet and scholar of the early Hellenistic age, born on Kos around 340 BCE. He was tutor to the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</a> and is regarded as the first poet-scholar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>, writing elegies and a glossary of rare words. Later poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocritus">Theocritus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a> admired him, but only fragments of his work survive.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of the life of Philitas, whom ancient sources call a Coan, a native or long-time inhabitant of Cos. His student <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocritus">Theocritus</a> gave his father's name as Telephos. The Suda yields an estimated birth about 340 BCE. Ptolemy had captured Cos in 310 BCE during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-diadochi">Wars of the Diadochi</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</a> was born there in 308 BCE. Philitas was appointed Philadelphus' tutor, which suggests he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> about 297/6 BCE and returned to Cos in the later 290s BCE. He also taught the poets Hermesianax and Theocritus and the grammarian Zenodotus, and after his return he seems to have led for at least ten years a brotherhood of intellectuals and poets that included Aratus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Philitas wrote a vocabulary of rare literary words, local dialect words and technical terms, probably a lexicon, called Disorderly Words. Only a few fragments survive. About thirty fragments of his poetry are known, with four definite titles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a>, his best-regarded work, was in elegiac couplets and seems to have told of Demeter's search for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a> and her welcome on Cos by its royal family of Meropids. Hermes was a short mythological narrative in hexameter, telling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>' visit to Aeolus, keeper of the winds, and his secret affair with the king's daughter Polymele. Playthings held shorter poems in the form of epigrams. At most fifty verses survive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Philitas seems to have died on Cos in the 280s BCE. He was the first person known to be called both poet and scholar, and as tutor to Philadelphus he is assumed to have influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mouseion">Mouseion</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. Theocritus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a> acknowledged his work as a classic, and he directly influenced Callimachus and Apollonius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>. Propertius linked him with Callimachus, and Quintilian ranked him second only to Callimachus among elegiac poets. Almost all his writing seems to have vanished within two centuries. A marble bust found at Crest in 1770, a Roman copy of a lost bronze, is thought to be his only portrait, and is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philitas of Cos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kos was a favoured base of the Ptolemaic Empire, where Ptolemy II was born and the poet <b>Philitas</b> taught.</blockquote><a href="/state/kos">Kos</a></li></ul></section>
